Looking at the commit message "lib: fix IP selection in DNS caching" and examining the code, I can see the issue is in the DNS caching functionality within the `DNSCaching` function in `attack.go`. 

The problem is in how IP addresses are selected when multiple IPs are returned from DNS resolution. The current implementation filters IPs to only include the first IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, but it doesn't properly handle the case where there might be fewer than 2 unique IP families or where the filtering logic is flawed.

Looking more carefully at the code, I see that in the `DNSCaching` function, there's a section that filters IPs to only include the first IPv4 and IPv6 addresses:

```go
// In place filtering of ips to only include the first IPv4 and IPv6.
j := 0
for i := 0; i < len(ips) && j < 2; i++ {
    ip := net.ParseIP(ips[i])
    switch {
    case len(ip) == net.IPv4len && (j == 0 || len(ips[j-1]) == net.IPv6len):
        fallthrough
    case len(ip) == net.IPv6len && (j == 0 || len(ips[j-1]) == net.IPv4len):
        ips[j] = ips[i]
        j++
    }
}
```

This logic is incorrect because it's comparing `len(ips[j-1])` which refers to the length of the string representation, not the IP address. The fix should properly compare IP families.

